    "evidence_text": "Prevention efforts might reduce HIV incidence but are unlikely to eliminate this disease.\n\nSouth Africa decided to adopt this strategy for pregnant and TB co-infected patients only.\n\nThis finding is robust to alternative assumptions and foreseeable changes in ART prices and effectiveness.\n\nAntiretroviral therapy has substantial potential to prevent HIV-associated tuberculosis.\n\nEleven studies met the inclusion criteria.\n\nCONCLUSIONS Antiretroviral therapy is strongly associated with a reduction in the incidence of tuberculosis across all CD4 count strata.\n\nIntegration of antiretroviral therapy with tuberculosis treatment.\n\nBACKGROUND We previously reported that integrating antiretroviral therapy (ART) with tuberculosis treatment reduces mortality.\n\nHowever, the timing for the initiation of ART during tuberculosis treatment remains unresolved.   \n\n\nIntervention scenarios systematically varied the CD4 count threshold for treatment eligibility, access to treatment, and programme retention.\n\nIntent-to-treat and on-treatment analyses were performed.\n\nAll patients were antiretroviral treatment-naive during treatment.   \n\n\nDeclining tuberculosis notification trend associated with strengthened TB and expanded HIV care in Swaziland.\n\nIn concentrated epidemics, expanded access among key populations was also cost-effective.   \n\n\nFUNDING The Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation and World Health Organization.",
